# Copy to .env.local (never committed) and fill in what you use. Everything is optional in
# development: without GITHUB_TOKEN the dev server asks the GitHub CLI (`gh auth login`) for one, and
# without GITHUB_CLIENT_ID the studio's "Sign in" borrows that same CLI login (only on your machine).

# ── Public building (/api/building) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Fine-grained tokens: repository access
# "Public repositories", no permissions. It only reads public data.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Studio sign-in (/studio) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The client ID of a GitHub App (recommended: read-only "Contents" and "Metadata") or an OAuth App.
# A client ID is public, not a secret. Step by step in docs/configuracao.md.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# The app's client secret, for the "Continue with GitHub" button. It is SECRET: put it only in
# .env.local (never committed) or in the host's environment (on Vercel, mark it Sensitive). The server
# uses it only to trade the sign-in code for a token; no session secret is needed. Without it, the
# studio signs in with GitHub's device flow instead (then "Enable Device Flow" must be ticked on the app).
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GitHub App only: the app's URL name (github.com/apps/<slug>). Public. With it, the studio shows a
# "Choose repositories on GitHub" button, since a GitHub App only reads the repos it is installed on.
GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# OAuth App only: scopes to ask for. `repo` reads private repos (GitHub only offers it as read and
# write); `public_repo` limits the studio to public ones. GitHub Apps ignore this.
GITHUB_OAUTH_SCOPE=repo
